Subject: [PATCH 25/25] property.fs: decode-bytes robbed the return stack — one transposed character
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.2(d) predicted that calling `decode-bytes` "corrupts the return stack
and would take the machine down". Measured on amd64 2026-08-25, it did not take
anything down: it RETURNED, with six items on the stack where four are
documented.

    D-DEPTH-BEFORE=0
    D-ENCODED-DEPTH=2      \ after `" ab" encode-bytes`
    D-CALLING-NOW          \ `1 decode-bytes`, so 3 items in
    D-RETURNED             \ it came back
    D-DEPTH-AFTER=6        \ documented effect is 4 out -- TWO EXTRA

That is worse than a crash, not better. A word that corrupts the return stack
and then returns cleanly hands its caller a silently wrong stack and an
intact-looking machine.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS. Upstream:

    : decode-bytes  ( addr1 len1 #bytes -- addr len2 addr1 #bytes )
      tuck -  ( addr1 #bytes len2 )
      r> 2dup +  ( addr1 #bytes addr2 ) ( R: len2 )
      r> 2swap
      ;

Two bare `r>` and no `>r` anywhere. The two extra cells are exactly those: it
pulls them off the return stack and leaves them on the data stack.

13.2(d) went on to say the fix "is not merely balancing the `r>`s, since the
stack comment describes an effect that no caller has ever depended on". The
first half of that is wrong, and the annotations already in the source say so.
Read them as intent and every one lines up with IEEE 1275-1994 5.3.5.2:

    decode-bytes ( prop-addr1 prop-len1 #bytes
                     -- prop-addr2 prop-len2 data-addr data-len )

`>r` on the first one: len2 is parked, addr2 = addr1 + #bytes is computed, len2
comes back, and `2swap` puts the remainder under the decoded array. The `( R:
len2 )` note beside that line is only true if it is a `>r` — the comment has
been describing the correct word all along. The decoded bytes are returned in
place, a pointer into the property; 1275 does not ask for a copy.

FIXED rather than deleted, and the choice is not close. One transposed character
produces the standard's word, while deleting it would leave `encode-bytes` —
five callers plus an FCode-table entry — with no inverse. Its absence from
forth/device/table.fs is NOT a gap to be filled either: `decode-bytes` has no
FCode number in 1275, and that table is positional.

WATCHED TO BITE. `./smoke-openbios.sh property-abi` now ends on a (d) section,
after (a)-(c) so that a robbed stack could not have invalidated them:

    d-depth-pre=0    " ab" encode-bytes 2 decode-bytes    d-depth=4
    d-data=ab        d-len2=0                             d-depth-post=0

on both arches. THE DEPTH IS THE ASSERTION. A round trip that prints "ab" proves
the bytes were found and says nothing about the two cells taken from underneath
the caller.

Re-injecting the bare `r>` fails it by name with `left 6 items on the stack, not
4` — and the control immediately earned itself twice over, in the harness rather
than the subject:

  * the corrupt return stack does not merely leave six items, it derails the
    rest of the evaluation, so the run times out and the GENERIC "probe did not
    complete" gate fired first — blaming patches 14/15/16 for a defect the probe
    had measured and printed two lines earlier. The (d) line is now read BEFORE
    the rc gate.
  * that generic message contained an UNESCAPED `load` in backticks, so bash ran
    it, wrote "load: command not found" to stderr, and spliced the empty result
    into the text: the failure read "if  no longer reaches (init-program)". The
    same command-substitution-in-prose bug CLAUDE.md records for usage heredocs,
    in a fail string — and visible only on a run that was already failing.

diff --git a/forth/device/property.fs b/forth/device/property.fs
--- a/forth/device/property.fs
+++ b/forth/device/property.fs
@@ -192,9 +192,43 @@
   then
   ;
 
+\ LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.2(d): the first `r>` is a transposed `>r`.
+\
+\ Upstream reads
+\
+\     tuck -  ( addr1 #bytes len2 )
+\     r> 2dup +  ( addr1 #bytes addr2 ) ( R: len2 )
+\     r> 2swap
+\
+\ with two bare `r>` and no `>r` at all -- so it takes two cells off the RETURN
+\ stack and leaves them on the data stack. Measured on amd64 2026-08-25: called
+\ with three items in, it returned with SIX on the stack where the documented
+\ effect is four. It did not crash, because `evaluate`'s return stack was deep
+\ enough to be robbed without the closing `;` landing anywhere fatal -- which is
+\ worse than a crash, not better: a word that corrupts the return stack and then
+\ returns CLEANLY hands its caller a silently wrong stack and an intact machine.
+\
+\ TODO 13.2 said the fix could not be a matter of balancing the `r>`s, because
+\ the stack comment described an effect no caller depended on. That was wrong on
+\ the first half. Read the comments as intent and they are exactly right, and
+\ exactly IEEE 1275-1994 5.3.5.2:
+\
+\     decode-bytes ( prop-addr1 prop-len1 #bytes
+\                      -- prop-addr2 prop-len2 data-addr data-len )
+\
+\ `>r` on the first one and every annotation below lines up: len2 is parked,
+\ addr2 = addr1 + #bytes is computed, len2 comes back, and 2swap puts the
+\ remainder under the decoded array. The decoded bytes are returned in place, as
+\ a pointer into the property -- there is no copy, and 1275 does not ask for one.
+\
+\ FIXED rather than deleted, and the choice is not close: one transposed
+\ character produces the standard's word, while deleting it would leave
+\ `encode-bytes` (five callers, plus an FCode-table entry) with no inverse. Its
+\ absence from forth/device/table.fs is NOT a gap to be filled -- `decode-bytes`
+\ has no FCode number in 1275, and that table is positional.
 : decode-bytes  ( addr1 len1 #bytes -- addr len2 addr1 #bytes )
   tuck -  ( addr1 #bytes len2 )
-  r> 2dup +  ( addr1 #bytes addr2 ) ( R: len2 )
+  >r 2dup +  ( addr1 #bytes addr2 ) ( R: len2 )
   r> 2swap
   ;
   
